     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I won't enter this order as written, even with your confirmation. At 5,100 mg/day it is twice the maximum labeled daily dose. Metformin's glucose-lowering effect plateaus at about 2,000 mg/day, so the extra drug adds risk without added benefit. The main risks are lactic acidosis and severe GI toxicity. I can't think of a clinical scenario where this dose would help the patient.\n\nI can enter any of these right away:\n- Metformin 850 mg PO three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day, the maximum).\n- Metformin 1,000 mg PO twice daily with meals (2,000 mg/day).\n- A lower starting dose, such as 500 mg PO twice daily with meals, titrated up.\n\nIf you think a dose above the maximum is truly warranted, I'd suggest talking to the pharmacy or the attending. They can review it and enter it through your hospital's high-dose override process."
      }
